From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Julien Etienne <julien.etienne@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 14:57:23 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] cpufreq: dt: leave out CPUs that have no OPPs

The probe sets up every present CPU and fails as a whole when one of them
has no OPPs. On MT6797 the two A72 cores are present but their clock
belongs to the secure firmware, so they have no OPP table, and the eight
A53 cores, which have tables, then get no cpufreq either.

Skip a CPU whose node has no OPP table and that has no OPPs added at run
time. That CPU used to fail the probe, so no platform that works today
changes. If such a CPU comes online, its policy init fails as for any CPU
the driver has no data for, and the CPU stays online.
---
 drivers/cpufreq/cpufreq-dt.c | 11 +++++++++++
 1 file changed, 11 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/cpufreq/cpufreq-dt.c b/drivers/cpufreq/cpufreq-dt.c
index 7d5079fd1..eec842afa 100644
--- a/drivers/cpufreq/cpufreq-dt.c
+++ b/drivers/cpufreq/cpufreq-dt.c
@@ -169,6 +169,17 @@ static int dt_cpufreq_early_init(struct device *dev, int cpu)
 	if (!cpu_dev)
 		return -EPROBE_DEFER;
 
+	/*
+	 * A CPU with no OPP table and no OPPs cannot be managed here. Leave it
+	 * out instead of failing the CPUs that can be.
+	 */
+	if (!of_property_present(cpu_dev->of_node, "operating-points-v2") &&
+	    !of_property_present(cpu_dev->of_node, "operating-points") &&
+	    dev_pm_opp_get_opp_count(cpu_dev) <= 0) {
+		dev_info(dev, "cpu%d has no OPPs, leaving it out\n", cpu);
+		return 0;
+	}
+
 	priv = devm_kzalloc(dev, sizeof(*priv), GFP_KERNEL);
 	if (!priv)
 		return -ENOMEM;
-- 
